      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hale named after lumberman C.D. Hale, who operated a camp on Hale Lake in the 1880s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hale_michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A flag station called Hale opened on the Detroit, Bay City & Alpena Railroad in 1886, giving the town its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hale%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hale’s post office opened January 17, 1889, becoming a vital hub for mail and communication as the settlement grew.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hale%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1944 the Iosco County Fair returned to Hale, the first such event since the mid‑1930s, reviving local tradition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hale_michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hale lies about 16 miles northwest of Tawas City and 21 miles northeast of West Branch, in Plainfield Township, Iosco County.",
